title: Script sessions
tags: [[Scripting the GUI]]
kind: reference
aliases: scripting question script-keys [[script keys]]
summary: What a running script is to the GUI — the workspaces it owns, its question bar, the keys it borrows and gives back, the four keys it can never take, and how it is stopped.


A ''script session'' is a process driving the GUI through `mf gui`
([[Scripting the GUI]]). What follows is what the GUI guarantees while one runs —
to you, who answer it, and to whoever writes it.

!! The question

A script asks with [[mf gui input]]: the question appears in ''its own bar'', just
above the status line, with the keys it accepts. A status or error message cannot
hide it. Press one of the keys shown, or type <<cmd "answer:send">> and the value in
the command input.

By convention the shipped scripts pair the arrow keys with the letters, the same way
everywhere:

|!Key |!Means |
|`y` or → |yes |
|`n` or ← |no |
|`m` or ↑ |mixed |
|`s` or ↓ |skip — leave the entry alone; for a folder, its whole subtree |
|`u` or Backspace |take the previous answer back |
|`q` |stop |

A script may also ask for a line of text ([[mf gui prompt]]): the question is then the
prompt of [[The command input]], which offers the script's candidates; Enter
answers, Escape cancels.

Only ''one'' question exists at a time, whatever the number of scripts: a second one is
refused, not queued ([[GUI design decisions]]).

!! Script keys

While a question is up, the keys it awaits are ''borrowed from the GUI'': they are
seen before every panel and global binding, so a script offering `y`, `n` and `s` is
never in conflict with an `n` bound in the panel underneath — and that panel cannot be
used meanwhile. A key typed in a text field is text, and reaches neither.

The ''script keys'' checkbox in the question bar gives them back
(<<key "script-keys:toggle" tab>>, `script-keys:toggle`). Unticked, every key means
again what it means in the panel; the question stays, its keys struck through, and
is answered with `answer:send`, or by ticking the box again. The choice holds from
one question to the next, but every ''new'' run starts with its keys live: a script is
never deprived, without anyone noticing, of the answers it waits for.

!! Reserved keys

Four keys are yours, and a script cannot take them. A question asking for one is
refused (`400`) before it is shown.

|!Key |!Does |
|Escape |stops the asking script |
|Backspace |goes back: answers `back` |
|<<key "command-input:focus command" ":">>, <<key "command-input:focus bash" "!">> |open the command input |
|<<key "script-keys:toggle" tab>> |flips the script keys |

Escape and Backspace are reserved as such; the others are whatever keys are bound to
`command-input:focus` and `script-keys:toggle`, so rebinding those moves the
reservation with them ([[Keybindings]]).

''Escape'' stops the run and everything it started: the script's whole process group
gets `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` if it is still there two seconds later. The command
blocked on the question dies with it, which releases the question.

A stopped script ''does not get to clean up'': it is being killed. So the GUI does it,
once the script is gone — it closes the workspaces the script opened, and gives each
slot that was showing one of them the workspace it showed when the script was
launched. A slot you pointed elsewhere meanwhile is left as you put it. Whatever the
script wrote before the stop stays written, and what else it had in progress — a
temporary file, a half-done step — is its loss: a script with a result to report or
work to finish offers its own quit key — `q`, in every shipped script — which it
receives as an ordinary answer and acts on before exiting.

Only a ''stopped'' script is cleaned up after. One that ends by itself, even on an
error, leaves what it chose to leave: `!mf gui workspace new` is a script too, and
the workspace it opens is the point of it.

''Backspace'' ends an answer, not the run. It answers the question with the value
`back`, and the script decides what that means, since only it knows what its last
answer wrote ([[Why going back is the script's business]]). The shipped walks undo
the answer exactly, through the event log, and ask it again; they show `u`, the
letter doing the same. A script with no way back asks its question again: the key
is never a silent no-op, and never read as ''stop''.

Both keys work this way only while the script keys are ticked. Unticked, Escape and
Backspace are the GUI's own again, and <<cmd "script:stop">> stops the script from
the command input.

!! The workspaces a script owns

A script started by the GUI owns the workspace it was launched from, and every
workspace it opens. Its question bar, its prompt in the command input and its
task-bar entry show ''only while one of those workspaces is in a visible slot'':
switching tab puts the question away instead of leaving it over unrelated panels.

* The keys follow the bar: while the question is put away its keys are not borrowed, and Escape does not stop a script whose question is off screen.
* A prompt put away gives the command input back — its own prompt, its draft, its history, Enter running commands — and what was typed as an answer is kept, and comes back with the focus when an owned workspace returns.
* The script keeps waiting throughout. Nothing is cancelled by looking elsewhere.

A question from a script the GUI did not start — one run from a terminal — belongs to
nobody, and is always shown.

!! Working, or waiting for you

In the task bar, a script ''spins'' while it works — next to its progress bar when it
reports one — and stops moving, saying ''your answer'', while it waits for a key or a
line. Motion is the signal: a progress bar that only advances between two questions
stands as still as a blocked one ([[Status bar and task bar]]).

!! Failure is never silent

* A script that exits with an error is reported in red on the status bar of the workspace it was launched from, with its name and exit code, besides the `[exit N]` line of that workspace's message log. Its output goes there too — not to a scratch workspace it opened, which is gone by then.
* A question that could not be ''asked'' — the GUI closed, a timeout, another question already up — ends the shipped scripts with the reason on the status bar, instead of being read as an Escape.
* A script killed while it waits releases its question: the next one is not refused for the rest of the session.
